<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miluo River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Miluo is a river in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flowing west into the Xiang River near Dongting Lake. According to tradition, the poet and Chu minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> drowned himself in it in about 278 BCE in despair at the fall of the Chu capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and the Dragon Boat Festival, held on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month, commemorates the attempt to rescue him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miluo-river.jpg" alt="Miluo River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MNXANL · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:201906_Miluo_River_in_Miluo_City.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
